      "id": 1,
      "text": "Woodway was founded in 1865 by Confederate veteran Burl Kendrick, who bought 320 acres on the eastern side of present‑day Woodway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://woodwaytexas.gov/woodway-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1901 Captain Kendrick deeded land for the first school, named Whitehall School, marking the town’s early commitment to education.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://woodwaytexas.gov/woodway-history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Whitehall School closed in 1918, followed by the local church’s closure in 1924, ending two central community hubs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://woodwaytexas.gov/woodway-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Woodway sits just southwest of Waco, making it part of the Waco Metropolitan Statistical Area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
